\viewkind4\pard\cf1\fs32\b Subnetting by hand: five steps\b0\fs22\par\par
\pard\sa120 Start here \u183? Worked example \u183? IPv4\par
\pard\sa120 Let\u8217?s work out \f1 192.168.10.42/26\f0  without a calculator. We want the network address, broadcast address and usable host range.\par
\pard\sa120 1Count the bits left for hostsAn IPv4 address has 32 bits. The prefix /26 uses 26 for the network. That leaves \b 32 \u8722? 26 = 6 host bits\b0 .\par
\pard\sa120 2Turn the bits into an address countEach bit has two choices: 0 or 1. Six bits give 2 \u215? 2 \u215? 2 \u215? 2 \u215? 2 \u215? 2 = \b 64 addresses\b0 . You can count by doubling: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64.\par
\pard\sa120 3Find the block containing 42For this /26, the first three octets stay fixed. The last octet is divided into blocks of 64: \b 0\u8211?63, 64\u8211?127, 128\u8211?191, 192\u8211?255\b0 . The number 42 is in 0\u8211?63, so the network is \b 192.168.10.0\b0 .\par
\pard\sa120 4Find the broadcast addressThe next network starts at .64. Step back one address: \b 192.168.10.63\b0  is the broadcast address of this block.\par
\pard\sa120 5Leave the endpoints out of the host rangeFor this conventional subnet, .0 identifies the network and .63 is broadcast. Hosts run from \b 192.168.10.1 to 192.168.10.62\b0 . That gives \b 64 \u8722? 2 = 62 usable hosts\b0 .\par
\pard\sa120 Network\b .0\b0 First host\b .1\b0 Last host\b .62\b0 Broadcast\b .63\b0 \par
\pard\sa120 The block-size shortcut must follow the octet where the mask changes. A /20 changes in the third octet, not the fourth. And /31, /32 and IPv6 need their own host-count rules.\par
\pard\sa120 Standard behind the method: \cf2\ul RFC 4632 \u8212? CIDR\ul0\cf1  (online: \cf2\ul https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc4632/\ul0\cf1 ).\par
